A home NAS instead of Google Drive or Dropbox
Off The Cloud is free, open-source software that turns a Raspberry Pi 5 with two USB drives into your family's own file storage: a NAS that keeps folders on your computers in sync, backs up your phones and shares files with a link, with no storage plan to pay for every month. The same device is your family's private social network and photo library.
@a.href=https://demo.off-the.cloud/
Try the demo Look around a real device with a made-up family's photos and files. No sign-up.
What you keep from a cloud drive
Folders in sync. The Mac, Windows and Linux apps keep the folders you choose in two-way sync with the device, or back them up one way, in the background.
Your files on your phone. Browse, open, upload and share them from the iPhone and Android apps, or from any browser in the web app.
Share links. Send files as a link that expires: whoever has it downloads them as a ZIP, with no account.
Nothing lost by mistake. Make a folder upload-only and nothing in it can be deleted, from any app; a file uploaded again under the same name keeps its earlier version.
Space for everyone. Each person in the family gets their own account, with their files encrypted under their own password.
Faster at home. At home the apps talk to the device over your own network, so big folders sync at Wi-Fi speed instead of your internet connection's upload speed.
What changes when your files come home
You buy the hardware once. A Raspberry Pi 5 and two USB drives, mirrored so that each keeps a full copy. Your space is what one drive holds; when it fills up, you swap in bigger drives, one at a time, without stopping.
A full backup is easy. The mirror protects you from a drive failing; for a copy kept somewhere else, take one drive out and put a new one in: the device fills the new one, and the one you took out is your backup.
Away from home, through the bridge. Your apps reach the device through our relay (free for two years, then €9.99 + VAT a year) or Tailscale Funnel, with no ports to open on your router, or through your own VPN.
More than a drive. The same device backs up every phone in the family in full resolution, keeps a photo library you can search, and runs your family's private social network.
