Which one should you choose?
Off The Cloud if you want your family's files at home, with no monthly plan, on a device that's easy to set up and also backs up your photos and runs your private social network.
Google Drive if you live in Google's apps and edit documents together in the browser; it has no Linux app, and its space is shared with Gmail and Google Photos.
Dropbox if you want the most familiar sync, Linux included, and don't mind a monthly plan; passwords and expiry dates on links need Essentials or a business plan.
iCloud Drive if everyone in your family uses Apple devices: it's built in, with nothing to set up.
Proton Drive if you want storage from a privacy-focused cloud company, with open-source apps; there's no Linux app yet.
pCloud or MEGA if you'd rather avoid a monthly fee or want plenty of free space: pCloud sells lifetime plans, and MEGA gives 20 GB free.
A Synology NAS if you want a traditional NAS, with network drives for your computers and many apps, and don't mind choosing its drives and setting it up.
Nextcloud if you already run a server and want calendar, contacts and office apps too: it's free and open source, but installing it, reaching it from outside and backing it up are up to you.
Moving your files over
From Google Drive: download everything with Google Takeout, which can turn Google Docs, Sheets and Slides into ordinary files, such as OpenDocument or PDF. Unzip it on a computer and add that folder to the Mac, Windows or Linux app.
From Dropbox, Proton Drive, pCloud and the like: if the service's app keeps a folder on your computer, make sure every file in it is downloaded, not online-only, then add that folder to the Mac, Windows or Linux app: every file goes to your device. Otherwise, download your files from the service's website first.
From iCloud Drive: on a Mac, copy the files into a folder of your own, outside iCloud Drive, and add that folder to the Mac app.
